  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nThe stubborn subtropical ridge remains anchored over the eastern \nFour Corners this afternoon. The influence of this feature continues \nto be the headline for the forecast, as above normal temperatures \nare expected to persist into next week. The ridge has been very \ngradually nudging east over the last day or so, and will continue to \ndo so through early next week. While it will remain quite hot, a \nvery subtle cooling trend continues into Tuesday, with high \ntemperatures falling about a degree each day. However, for today, \nhigh temperatures remain about 10 degrees above normal, topping out \nin the upper 90s to low 100s for the inland valleys, upper 80s and \nlow 90s for the western valleys, and 105-117 degrees in the deserts. \nThe weak easterly flow aloft with the ridge setup is also keeping \nmoisture slightly elevated, leading to the continually mild \nnighttime temperatures. The cumulative combination of the high \ntemperatures and warm nights keeps HeatRisk elevated despite subtle \ncooling compared to a few days ago, with widespread moderate (level \n2/4) HeatRisk in and west of the mountains, with major (level 3/4) \nHeatRisk in the deserts. As the ridge slowly nudges eastward and the \ngradual cooling trend ensues into Tuesday, HeatRisk follows suit, \nwith slight improvements each day. The Extreme Heat Warning (for the \ndeserts, Inland Empire, and San Diego County mountains) and Heat \nAdvisory (everywhere else) remains in effect through 8 PM Monday \nevening. \n\nAs the ridge begins to nudge eastward through the next few days, \nthis opens the door for some monsoonal influences to sneak in. While \nthe subsidence will not be as strong, PWATs aren't overly \nimpressive. The general expectation is for afternoon cumulus \ndevelopment over the mountains for Sunday-Tuesday. PoPs continue to \nbe quite meager with Sunday having the best \"shot\" at seeing any \nshowers over the mountains (~5% or less). \n\nTuesday into the end of next week...\n\nWith the upper ridge displaced some towards the front range of the \nRockies and the southwestern Great Plains on Tuesday/Wednesday, high \ntemperatures will be moderated compared to what we're expecting this \nweekend, but will still run about 5 degrees above normal. Even \nworse, Tues/Wed will be the \"coolest\" days of the week, as ensemble \nclusters all agree that the ridge will slowly migrate back west \nthrough the Desert Southwest and strengthen in the latter half of \nthe week. The ridge looks to become centered over Las Vegas by \nSaturday, with some ensemble members taking the strength up to 600dm \nat 500mb. This setup could very well bring the hottest afternoon \ntemperatures of the Summer so far, with some daytime temperature \nrecords potentially being challenged. Palm Springs currently has a \n20-30% chance of seeing highs >120 degrees next Saturday, with the \nInland Empire having about a 20% chance of seeing highs >110 \ndegrees. Additional heat headlines are looking increasingly likely \nfor the second half of next week.",
